10.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The line et tibi bene esse soli quom sibi sit male is being discussed under conflicting metrical hypotheses. Syllable inventory is broadly agreed: et ti bi be ne es se so li quom si bi sit ma le, 15 syllables, 14 with the conventional synalepha/elision bene esse > be-nes-se.

Quantities that are stable across most colleagues:
* et L by position before t-;
* ti S, bi S open short;
* so L by nature, li S open short;
* quom L by position/closed -m-;
* si S, bi S open short;
* sit L by position -t-;
* ma is disputed long by nature vs short by nature; le final is treated as long in classical hexameter closure but short in strict natural quantity.

Points of disagreement:
* Elision/synalepha of bene esse. Colleagues 10,12,8,11 treat be S nes L se S with ne+es merged, giving be-nes-se. Colleagues 3,4,2,10 also allow a school scansion where be is read long and es long by position, leading to a forced dactylic shape. Colleague 1,13,14,5,7,9 give various 6-foot iambic/trochaic groupings without consistent elision.
* Metrical genre. 10,12,8 argue the line is famously unmetrical/defective for dactylic hexameter: the second and third feet are S S L / S L S with hiatus bene esse, first foot et ti bi is a perfect dactyl L S S, fourth foot quom si bi is L S S, and the line is quoted as an illustration of a spoiled hexameter. 11,12,10 note an alternative 14-position iambic septenarius reading with anacrusis et → L S S L etc., the usual proverbial septenarius scansion. 3,4,2 claim a regular dactylic hexameter can be obtained with synalepha be-nes-se and school long readings for es-se/so-li, giving L S S | L S S | L S S | L S S | L L S / L S L.
* Foot division and notation. With elision the syllable list is et L ti S bi S be S nes L se S so L li S quom L si S bi S sit L ma ? le ?. This yields the common syllable-by-syllable pattern L S S S L S L S L S S L L ?, which is then grouped differently: 10 → L S S | S S L | S L S | L S S | L L L on strict quantity, becoming L S S | S L S | L S L | S S L | L S with elision; 12 → L S S | S L S | L S L | S S L | L S; 11 → anacrusis + iambic septenarius L S S L L S L S... etc.

Unresolved questions for me: exact quantity of ma in male, final length of le in the line, how the question wants foot boundaries: dactylic 6 feet vs septenarius 7 feet vs simple syllable string. The elision bene esse is almost universally assumed in the classical discussion, but the resulting second foot be-nes-se is anapaestic S L S, not dactylic/spondaic, which is why the line is cited as defective. I will keep both the strict syllable quantities and the two competing foot groupings, and note the be/ne/es quantity dispute and the ma/le nature/position dispute as the remaining decision points.

